> Summary 1:
> 
> Economic Impacts
> Increased Economic Losses:
> Economic damages from extreme weather events, particularly flooding and wildfires, have risen significantly. Historical precipitation changes have contributed to about one-third of flood damages from 1988 to 2017 (10.1073/pnas.2017524118). Wildfires also lead to substantial economic losses and are expected to increase in intensity and frequency (10.1088/1748-9326/aba868; 10.1007/s10584-019-02558-8).
> By the end of the century, annual AR-related flood damages could increase from $1 billion to $2.3 billion under RCP4.5 or $3.2 billion under RCP8.5 (10.1038/s41598-022-15474-2).
> Sector-Specific Financial Impacts:
> The crop insurance losses have increased due to global warming, with a 19% contribution to national crop insurance losses between 1991-2017 (10.1088/1748-9326/ac1223).
> Climate change affects freshwater availability and can significantly impact water resources management, with consumptive use potentially decreasing by 8% or increasing by 235% over the next 50 years (10.1029/2021EF002222).
> Agricultural Impacts
> Crop Yields and Production:
> Heat stress negatively impacts crop yield, particularly for crops like maize, soybean, and wheat, with projected decreases in yield under future climate scenarios (10.1088/1748-9326/ac15ce; 10.1007/s11069-020-04484-w).
> Changes in the climate have variable effects on agricultural insect pests, increasing future insecticide use by 4-20% by 2050 due to landscape and climate changes (10.1007/s10980-020-01130-5).
> Irrigation and Water Stress:
> Water limitations due to climate stress can affect crops significantly. Projections indicate a need for increased irrigation and adaptation strategies to counterbalance decreases in yield (10.1088/1748-9326/acf146).
> Human Health Impacts
> Effects of Air Quality and Heat:
> Ozone exposure, compounded by climate change, is associated with asthma-related emergency department visits, predicting a significant net health and economic benefit of climate mitigation (10.1016/j.envres.2020.109206).
> The incidence of climate-related health conditions, including heat-related illnesses and respiratory issues due to wildfires and poor air quality, is increasing (10.1016/j.joclim.2021.100110; 10.5194/acp-20-14547-2020).
> Environmental and Ecological Impacts
> Biodiversity and Ecosystem Dynamics:
> Climate aects biodiversity through changes in species distributions and ecosystem services, exacerbating the vulnerability of ecosystems (10.1007/s10584-020-02940-x; 10.1038/s43247-021-00123-9).
> Coastal and Marine Systems:
> Rising sea levels threaten coastal areas, leading to wetland losses with substantial economic valuations attached to ecosystem services lost as wetlands decline (10.1016/j.ocecoaman.2022.106248).
> Socioeconomic and Broader Impacts
> Infrastructure and Built Environment:
> Climate variability can disrupt supply chains, impact infrastructure reliability, and increase the need for resilient design in transportation and energy sectors (10.1088/1748-9326/ac998d; 10.1007/s43630-023-00375-8).
> This synthesis highlights the multifaceted nature of climate impacts across economic, agricultural, health, environmental, and socioeconomic dimensions in the U.S. The abstracts emphasize the importance of considering both current and future impacts to guide mitigation and adaptation strategies effectively.
